{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793809573,"nonce":"j/YYZYGiPIaeH7pMepYjKJJ4IOg=","title":"OSR: retain non-null live-ins and scalarize loops with opaque initial objects","message":"The interpreter-first dispatch change exposes op_overload's lowered-frame OSR path. Its source constructor returns a non-null allocation, but outlining discards that proof; sign_fold also ignores parameter nonnull attributes. This leaves nil/fallback paths in the region. Even after those paths disappear, partial_escape refuses a single allocation combined with an opaque incoming object, or an allocation web without consuming escapes. Both shapes are natural for a loop entered from an interpreter frame.\n\nInfer return proofs from available bodies on the compilation worker, preserve only proven non-null live-ins through outline, and consume those parameter attributes in shared simplification. Reuse the existing guarded scalar/backing-pointer representation for single-allocation and no-escape webs. Preserve nullable backedges, aliasing, opaque-input mutation, pointer identity observations and real cold exits. Do not assign noalias to OSR parameters or change the snapshot format.\n\nThis is a bounded part of 60a9e71ab07f59f416ea7336ab573e807e1f068d05cf91ca64cdeb163a4b9b19. Allocation/global disjointness lost by method_call remains separate. Validate both JIT tiers, interpreter semantics, full pinned TypeScript corpus, and thermally gated NUC wall time with no builds during samples.","files":null}]}